--
std::string s("ab") -- construction from a string literal.

Front-end non-conformance: basic_string<char> is truncated at its
const_reverse_iterator member typedef (reverse_iterator<const_iterator>, whose
eager definition-instantiation throws via the C++20 iterator-concept chain),
dropping its constructors, so it is misclassified as a POD and s("ab") fails with
a char[3] -> basic_string CONVERSION ERROR.  N5008 [temp.inst]/1-2,
[dcl.typedef], [class.prop].

A demonstrated un-truncation fix makes this conformant (reaches BMC), but its
blanket form makes many container tests' BMC intractable; the
conformant-AND-tractable fix is the [temp.inst]/2 declarations-vs-definitions
split (pending).  Reclassify to CORE once that lands and std::string
construction is BMC-tractable.
See doc/architectural/cpp-class-template-instantiation-plan.md.
